<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moulay Ismail</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1645–<a href="/years/1727">1727</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Ismail ibn Sharif · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty">Alaouite dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moulay Ismail was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> from 1672 to 1727, the second ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty">Alaouite dynasty</a>. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meknes">Meknes</a> a vast imperial city and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-guard-morocco">Abid al-Bukhari</a>, an army of enslaved black soldiers. He won back coastal strongholds held by Europeans, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehdya">La Mamora</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larache">Larache</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, which the English abandoned in 1684.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moulay-ismail.jpg" alt="Moulay Ismail" width="320" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Germain Moüette · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ismail_Ibn_Sharif.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Moulay Ismail was a son of Sharif ibn Ali, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a> and first sovereign of the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty">Alawi dynasty</a>, whose clan claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> through Hassan Ad-Dakhil. According to the historian Ibn Zaydan, his mother Mubarka was a black slave of the Saharan Mghafra tribe. He governed the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> from 1667 until the death of his half-brother Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-rashid-of-morocco">Moulay Rashid</a> in 1672.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Proclaimed sultan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, Ismail faced rebellions from his nephew Moulay Ahmed ben Mehrez, who claimed the throne until his death in 1687, from his brothers, one of whom took the title King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a>, and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tetouan</a> warlord Khadir Ghaïlan. Around 1677 he began to assert his authority over the whole country. He built his power on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-guard-morocco">Abid al-Bukhari</a>, or Black Guard, black slaves devoted to him, and on the jaysh tribes, so that the central power relied less on tribes that often rebelled.</p><p>He expelled Europeans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larache">Larache</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asilah">Asilah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehdya">Mehdya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, took thousands of Christians prisoner and nearly took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>. Corsairs based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a> supplied him with European captives and weapons. He had mixed fortunes against Ottoman Algeria: a large army sent in 1692 was defeated, the Algerians advanced as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, and the border was fixed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulouya-river">Moulouya River</a>. His army was routed at the Battle of Chelif in 1701, and a treaty of that year held until his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ismail made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meknes">Meknes</a> his capital in 1672 and built a citadel and palace complex there, with gates, mosques, gardens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and more than forty kilometres of walls. Fittings were stripped from the Saadian El Badi Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> and marble was taken from the Roman ruins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a>. At least 25,000 workers were employed, mostly paid labourers and some conscripted Christian prisoners. Europeans compared him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, and called him the bloody king for his cruelty to Christian slaves.</p><p>He died on 22 March 1727 after an illness, having reigned 55 years, the longest reign of any sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. Chroniclers such as Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Nasiri describe his rule as a period of peace after nearly a century of division. After his death his supporters grew so powerful that they enthroned and deposed sultans at will.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ismail Ibn Sharif” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Moulay Ismail</b>, ruling from 1672 to 1727, made Meknes a vast imperial city and built the Abid al-Bukhari, an army of enslaved black soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/state/alaouite-sultanate">Alaouite Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The garrison was under constant pressure from Moroccan forces, especially after <b>Moulay Ismail</b> of the Alaouite Sultanate came to power.</blockquote><a href="/state/english-tangier">English Tangier</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meknes">Meknes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.32,-4.26&amp;year=1698" aria-label="Tafilalt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Moulay Ismail"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4689 4881h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4689 4881h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.32,-4.26&amp;year=1698">What was here in 1698? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
